=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — that Office-department machines (192.168.3.x) cannot
reach Internet-Lab machines (128.168.0.x) because the inter-router WAN link
is down — does not correspond to any observable fault in the network.

Every element of the alleged fault path was inspected and found healthy.
The WAN link between router1 (eth3, 20.20.0.1/8) and router2 (eth2,
20.20.0.2/8) on the wan_r1_r2 segment is in LOWER_UP / UP state on both
sides. RIP version 2 is running and correctly configured on all three
routers: router1 advertises its four directly-connected networks
(192.168.3.0/24, 192.168.4.0/24, 10.0.0.0/8, 20.0.0.0/8); router2
advertises its three (1.0.0.0/8, 128.168.0.0/16, 20.0.0.0/8); full
bidirectional route tables are installed on every router. End-host gateways
are correct (office_pc → 192.168.3.1; internet-lab PCs → 128.168.0.1).
Pings from office_pc to all four Internet-Lab hosts (pc2–pc5,
128.168.0.2–.5) return 0% packet loss at round-trip times of 1–3 ms.

Root cause: No fault exists. The ticket is a false premise — the user
incorrectly concluded the WAN link had failed, most likely from a transient
application-layer issue (e.g., a server process that was briefly down) or a
local ARP/cache miss that self-resolved. No corrective action is required on
the network infrastructure.
=== END ===
